you try:

gcc main.c function.c -o prog

by itself it won't work, because the "<>" type include is trying to find the function in the compilers configured include search paths, so you might have to add the current paths using "-I."

you can check the current directory with "". if you use "" you will not need to include the "-I."

when you use CMake it will add include directories (let's say you have an Inc dir, then if you write):

        include_directories(Inc)

It will add that directory to the search paths. (-IInc) (keep in mind that your lsp(?) (like clangd and stuff)  will not pick up on this)

So you can use <> includes in Cmake just fine, but the problem is that this defies the convention.

usually "" means "own project files" and <> means "external/system library files"

so it's usually better not to break this convention


